{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790274825,"nonce":"yTgbt0LXfTJa4Xk9Hzt9cwQUVnw=","title":"Lua: bracketed string keys in a table constructor keep their escapes unprocessed","message":"{['\\n'] = 'N'} stores the key as the two raw source bytes backslash and n (92, 110), not a newline; the same for ['\\65'], ['\\x42'], ['\\u{43}']. A runtime key such as string.char(10) or a gsub capture then misses silently, which breaks escape tables in JSON, CSV and HTML encoders. Found by the Lua 5.4 audit at 8844ca79 on both tiers. The string literal path for keys must go through the same escape decoding as expression string literals.","files":null}]}